  ],
  "research": {
    "title": "Tektronix 11401 Digitizing Oscilloscope",
    "year": "1986",
    "by": "Tektronix (Beaverton, OR)",
    "tags": ["HCI", "Scientific Instrument", "Touchscreen", "Front Panel", "Digitizing Oscilloscope"],
    "overview": "The Tektronix 11401 and its siblings the 11402 (1 GHz) and 11403 (3 GHz color, 1989) were digitizing oscilloscopes that replaced the 7K series in 1986 with a completely refreshed user interface built around 'a touch screen and menu system.' As vintageTEK puts it, 'As such there are few controls on the mainframe or plug-ins.' Where earlier scopes were walls of knobs and buttons, the 11K-series operator steered nearly everything through a touchscreen and on-screen menus, with a small set of dedicated knobs and bezel soft-keys around a large display.\n\nThe interaction model is the museum's clearest 'instrument front panel as graphical interface' — a laboratory tool whose operating surface was software-defined by touch, a decade and a half before touchscreens reached consumer gadgets. Project team lead Tom Rousseau had also led the museum's Tektronix 7854, making the 7854 → 11401 line a direct evolution from a knob-and-soft-key computer scope to a touchscreen-menu instrument; assistant project manager Murlan Kaufman was specifically 'the person responsible for the touch panel and display.'",
    "deepDive": [
      {
        "heading": "The front panel that became a GUI",
        "text": "The 11401 reduced the physical control surface to a near-blank face: a display surrounded by a few soft-keys and dedicated knobs, with channel, trigger, and measurement setup driven from on-screen touch menus. This is the essence of a software-defined instrument — the operator negotiates the machine through a touchscreen rather than a fixed array of switches, the same philosophical leap that turned telephone exchanges, cash registers, and later cars into software-defined devices."
      },
      {
        "heading": "From 7854 to 11401",
        "text": "Tektronix's 7854 (already in the museum) bolted a Reverse-Polish-Notation waveform keyboard onto an analog scope; the 11401 went further and replaced the fixed panel with a touchscreen menu system. The pair bookend the transition from discrete-symbolic controls (knobs + RPN keyboard) to direct-manipulation graphical instrumentation, sharing the same project lineage under Tom Rousseau."
      }
    ],
